sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch14-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "There is no subtle mystery in phyllotaxis: the steady production of similar parts, similarly placed, at similar successive intervals of time necessarily produces symmetrical patterns (helices on a cylinder, logarithmic spirals on a surface containing the pole), which are a secondary mathematical result rather than a primary phenomenon.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Phyllotactic spirals follow inevitably from similar parts produced at regular intervals";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XIV, “On Leaf-Arrangement, or Phyllotaxis”; topic “Church's theory of phyllotaxis”. Scope: phyllotaxis of leaves, florets, bracts and scales"
}
